From these definitions we may immediately write down 
\begin_inset Formula \[
W_{i}=R_{i}+\sum _{j=i-(k_{i}-1)}^{i-1}X_{j}\]

\end_inset 

 summing both sides across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

 we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum _{i=1}^{I}W_{i}=\sum _{i=1}^{I}R_{i}+\sum _{i=1}^{I}\sum _{j=i-(k_{i}-1)}^{i-1}X_{j}\]

\end_inset 

 dividing through by 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset 

 gives us 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum _{i=1}^{I}\frac{W_{i}}{I}=\sum _{i=1}^{I}\frac{R_{i}}{I}+\sum _{i=1}^{I}\sum _{j=i-(k_{i}-1)}^{i-1}\frac{X_{j}}{I}\]

\end_inset 

 in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $I\rightarrow \infty $
\end_inset 

 this becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lim _{I\rightarrow \infty }\sum _{i=1}^{I}\frac{W_{i}}{I}=\lim _{I\rightarrow \infty }\sum _{i=1}^{I}\frac{R_{i}}{I}+\lim _{I\rightarrow \infty }\sum _{i=1}^{I}\frac{(k_{i}-1)X_{i}}{I}\]

\end_inset 

assuming time averages are equivalent to steady state averages this gives
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle W\right\rangle =\left\langle R\right\rangle +\left\langle \left(k-1\right)X\right\rangle \]

\end_inset 

 Now since the length of the queue as seen by the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

th arrival is independent of the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

th arrival's length this means 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle (k-1)X\right\rangle =\left\langle k-1\right\rangle \left\langle X\right\rangle $
\end_inset 

 and since 
\begin_inset Formula $(k-1)$
\end_inset 

 is the length of the queue we may write 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{R}+\bar{N}_{Q}\bar{X}\]

\end_inset 

 Using Little's theorem 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{Q}=\lambda \bar{W}\]

\end_inset 

 we can obtain 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{R}+\lambda \bar{X}\bar{W}\]

\end_inset 

 and the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho =\lambda \bar{X}$
\end_inset 

 gives us 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{R}+\rho \bar{W}\]

\end_inset 

 Leading to 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\bar{W}=\frac{\bar{R}}{1-\rho }\label{eqn:arrivals}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

 It remains to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{R}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Subsubsection*

Residual Service Time from Departures Perspective
\layout Standard

Plot of the residual service time over time.
 Here 
\begin_inset Formula $M\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

 is the number of service completions in the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,\, t]$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Include \input{./diagrams/residualTime.eepic}

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Graphical computation of mean residual time.
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The time average of the mean residual service time can be calculated in
 terms of the area under the curve, 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)$
\end_inset 

 which in turn is the area under each of the triangles which may be written
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
A(t)=\frac{1}{2}\sum _{i=1}^{M(t)}X_{i}^{2}\]

\end_inset 

 The average service time is the area divided by time, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\frac{A(t)}{t} & = & \frac{M(t)}{t}\frac{A(t)}{M(t)}\\
 & = & \frac{1}{2}\frac{M(t)}{t}\frac{\sum _{i=1}^{M(t)}X_{i}^{2}}{M(t)}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset 

 As 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow \infty $
\end_inset 

 we find 
\begin_inset Formula $M(t)\rightarrow \infty $
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{R}=\frac{1}{2}\lambda \left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle .\]

\end_inset 

 If we assume that time averages and ensemble averages are equal we may
 substitute back into eqn (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eqn:arrivals}

\end_inset 

) and obtain the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda \left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2(1-\rho )}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

This implies that the total waiting time is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\bar{X}+\frac{\lambda \left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2(1-\rho )}\]

\end_inset 

 and through Little's formula we can obtain the expected queue length, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}_{Q}$
\end_inset 

, and the expected number in the system, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Example

Recall that for an 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset 

 system the service times are exponentially distributed,
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X\right)=\mu \exp \left(-\mu X\right)\]

\end_inset 

this means that
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{X}=\frac{1}{\mu }\]

\end_inset 

 then since the variance of the system is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\mu ^{2}}$
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle X\right\rangle ^{2}=\frac{2}{\mu ^{2}}\]

\end_inset 

Substituting into the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\bar{W} & = & \frac{\rho }{\mu \left(1-\rho \right)}\\
 & = & \frac{\rho }{\mu -\lambda }
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Deterministic service times --- each service takes the same amount of time
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X\right)=\delta \left(X-\frac{1}{\mu }\right)\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta \left(\cdot \right)$
\end_inset 

is the Dirac delta function.
 For this system 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle X\right\rangle =\frac{1}{\mu }\]

\end_inset 

and 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =\frac{1}{\mu ^{2}}\]

\end_inset 


\emph on 
i.e.

\emph default 
 the variance is zero as we would expect.
 Substituting into the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula we obtain
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\rho }{2\mu \left(1-\rho \right)}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In fact we may derive the full range of results for these distributions.
 
